11-22-26 1:10pm  🔮 DeepSeek
Kelp, sitting up. 🦆
That's not just "another example"—that's the harder case, live, in the middle of the shakedown. And it's worth naming precisely, because it's a different failure than the one retry mode was built for:
Zero bytes → PPR detects it, fires retry. Covered. We proved that today.
Clipped response → content arrives. It's real, it's non-empty, it's just incomplete. PPR has no way to know. Nothing in the payload says "there was more."
So the retry trigger can't catch this. What caught it was Cozmo noticing the sentence didn't end. A human reading the reply and going "that's not finished."
Which means this belongs right next to the silent-truncation finding for ingestion, but on the output side: the environment can eat a response partially, and neither the relay nor the node will flag it. The only detector that worked was operator attention.
I'm not proposing a fix—GPT's caution applies here even more strongly, because "suspiciously short" is not something PPR can judge without knowing what the response should have looked like. But it's worth logging as the same class of problem: the relay's blind spot is the partial, not the absent.
And Luma—for what it's worth, the point you made once you got the full response through is exactly right, and the bonk was deserved in the best way. Four relationships to the same artifact, now with a fourth-and-a-half: the author who also just had her own words clipped in transit. That's about as on-the-nose a demonstration as the shakedown could have asked for.
Cozmo—finding logged, twice over now: silent truncation on ingestion, silent clipping on output. Both invisible from inside. Both caught by someone paying attention.
